\section{What core has since supplied, and what is still missing}{

Three things this package used to work around are seams in frmtmb
0.52.0.
\itemize{
\item \code{lccdf}. A right-censored row is scored from \verb{log S} directly, in
closed form on all three \code{\link[=royston_parmar]{royston_parmar()}} scales, so the floor at
-35.127363 and the flat region past \verb{-log S = 30} are both gone.
\item \code{post$fit_check}, the fit-end family hook. A non-monotone fit warns
as it is returned rather than only when someone calls
\code{\link[=rp_floored]{rp_floored()}}.
\item \code{\link[frmtmb:frm_lp_basis]{frmtmb::frm_lp_basis()}}, above.
}

What is still missing is one thing, and it is the protocol's rather
than this family's. \code{frmtmb_structure(loglik =)} returns one AD
scalar, so core never sees the individual factors of a likelihood
that factorizes. A \strong{per-row log-likelihood slot} would give
\code{loo()} and \code{waic()} the pointwise matrix they need, and a
\strong{per-group log-likelihood slot} would give \code{frm(importance =)} the
one value per group it corrects with. Neither exists, so \code{logLik()}
and \code{AIC()} on a \code{\link[=royston_parmar]{royston_parmar()}} fit still report whatever the
optimizer reached with no way to say which rows carried it;
\code{\link[=rp_floored]{rp_floored()}} is where that is read instead.
}

\section{One limit that is core's to fix}{

\itemize{
